-- Migration: Question generation — AUD batch 48 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-23
-- Topic: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L3 — Qualitative factors in misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Meridian Construction Co., the engagement team identifies a $12,000 uncorrected misstatement in revenue recognition. While this amount is below the $75,000 materiality threshold, the misstatement converts what would otherwise be a covenant violation into covenant compliance. Under AU-C 450, what is the most likely effect of this qualitative factor on the auditor''s evaluation?', '["The misstatement should be waived because it is quantitatively immaterial", "The auditor should increase the materiality threshold to accommodate the misstatement", "The misstatement may be treated as material despite being below the quantitative threshold", "The misstatement must be reclassified as a projected misstatement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor must consider both quantitative and qualitative factors when evaluating uncorrected misstatements. A misstatement that affects debt covenant compliance is qualitatively significant because it masks a contractual breach, potentially making it material regardless of its dollar amount. Choice A is incorrect because quantitative immateriality does not override qualitative significance when the misstatement affects compliance with loan agreements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Prior-period uncorrected misstatements carried forward
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Brightstone Electronics had $18,000 in uncorrected inventory overstatements from the prior year that reversed in the current period. The auditor for the current year should treat these prior-period misstatements by:', '["Ignoring them because they were evaluated in the prior audit", "Including their current-period effect in the summary of uncorrected misstatements", "Requiring management to restate prior-period financial statements", "Reporting them as a significant deficiency to the audit committee"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.06, the auditor must evaluate the effect of uncorrected misstatements from prior periods on the current period''s financial statements. Prior-period misstatements that reverse create an opposite effect in the current period, which must be included in the current summary of uncorrected misstatements. Choice A is incorrect because prior-period misstatements do not lose relevance simply because they were previously evaluated — their reversal effect on current-period statements requires assessment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L2 — Clearly trivial threshold application
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Falcon Industries sets overall materiality at $100,000. The auditor establishes a clearly trivial threshold at $3,000. During testing, an error of $2,500 is found. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Not accumulate the misstatement because it is below the clearly trivial threshold", "Accumulate the misstatement on the summary of uncorrected misstatements", "Communicate the misstatement to those charged with governance immediately", "Reduce the clearly trivial threshold to capture the error"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, misstatements below the clearly trivial threshold need not be accumulated because they are inconsequential both individually and in the aggregate. The $2,500 error falls below the $3,000 threshold, so the auditor is not required to track it. Choice B is incorrect because AU-C 450 only requires accumulation of misstatements that are not clearly trivial, and this amount falls below that cutoff.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Deficiency in design vs deficiency in operation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Lakeview Medical Group, the auditor discovers that a control requiring supervisory review of claims processing exists in the policy manual. However, the supervisor assigned to perform the review has never been trained and routinely approves batches without examination. This situation represents:', '["A deficiency in the design of the control", "An inherent limitation of internal control", "A compensating control failure", "A deficiency in the operation of the control"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A1, a deficiency in operation exists when a properly designed control does not operate as intended or the person performing the control lacks the competence to do so effectively. Because the supervisory review control is documented in policy but the supervisor fails to execute it properly, this is an operational deficiency. Choice A is incorrect because the control''s design — requiring supervisory review — is appropriate; the failure lies in how the control is being carried out.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Summary of uncorrected misstatements purpose
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is the primary purpose of the auditor''s summary of uncorrected misstatements?', '["To document all adjusting entries proposed to management", "To accumulate identified misstatements for aggregate evaluation against materiality", "To serve as a formal communication to those charged with governance", "To calculate the projected misstatement for each account balance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.05, the auditor accumulates identified misstatements during the audit, other than those that are clearly trivial, so they can be evaluated in the aggregate to determine whether they are material to the financial statements. Choice A is incorrect because the summary tracks uncorrected misstatements specifically, not all proposed adjusting entries — corrected misstatements are removed from the evaluation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Written representations about uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After finalizing the summary of uncorrected misstatements for the audit of Ridgeline Consulting Group, the auditor requests management to provide written representations. Under AU-C 450, management''s written representation regarding uncorrected misstatements should state that:', '["All misstatements have been corrected prior to report issuance", "No additional misstatements exist beyond those identified by the auditor", "Management believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole", "Management acknowledges the existence of uncorrected misstatements but defers to the auditor''s materiality judgment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.14, the auditor must obtain written representations from management that state the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of these items is included with or attached to the representation letter. Choice D is incorrect because management cannot defer its responsibility — AU-C 450 requires management to make an affirmative representation about immateriality, not merely acknowledge the misstatements exist.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Iron curtain vs rollover approach judgment
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Cornerstone Retail Group, the auditor identifies a $40,000 depreciation understatement originating in the prior year that was not corrected. Using the rollover approach, the current-year income statement effect is zero because the error has fully reversed. However, the iron curtain approach shows the balance sheet remains misstated by $40,000. Overall materiality is $50,000. The auditor should evaluate this situation by:', '["Considering both the iron curtain and rollover effects and concluding the balance sheet misstatement requires attention because the rollover approach alone may not capture the cumulative impact on financial position", "Relying solely on the rollover approach because the current-year income statement is fairly stated", "Classifying the misstatement as clearly trivial because neither approach produces a material income statement effect", "Requiring management to restate the prior-year financial statements before issuing the current opinion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A16, the auditor should consider both the iron curtain approach (cumulative balance sheet effect) and the rollover approach (current-year income effect) when evaluating uncorrected misstatements. Relying solely on the rollover approach could overlook a material balance sheet misstatement that persists from prior periods. Choice B is incorrect because focusing only on the income statement effect ignores the $40,000 cumulative misstatement in the balance sheet, which approaches the $50,000 materiality threshold.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Risk of material misstatement definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 200, the risk of material misstatement refers to the risk that:', '["The auditor will issue an inappropriate opinion on the financial statements", "Internal controls will fail to prevent errors in transaction processing", "Management will intentionally manipulate the financial statements", "The financial statements are materially misstated prior to the audit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 200.14, the risk of material misstatement is the risk that the financial statements are materially misstated prior to the audit. It consists of inherent risk and control risk at the assertion level. Choice A is incorrect because it describes audit risk, which is the broader risk that the auditor expresses an inappropriate opinion — audit risk encompasses both the risk of material misstatement and detection risk.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: hard / L3 — IT general control deficiency cascading to application controls
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pinnacle Financial Services, the auditor discovers that the IT department has not restricted access to the production environment, allowing developers to make unreviewed changes to the accounting application. Three application controls — automated revenue cutoff, journal entry validation, and segregation of duties enforcement — depend on this application. What is the most likely effect of this IT general control deficiency on the auditor''s approach?', '["The auditor should test only the application controls that directly affect material account balances", "The auditor should treat all three dependent application controls as ineffective and expand substantive testing across the affected transaction cycles", "The auditor should request management to implement a compensating detective control before further testing", "The auditor should limit the scope of the audit to manual controls only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 315.A67, IT general controls support the continued proper functioning of application controls. When an IT general control such as access restriction over the production environment is deficient, the auditor cannot rely on dependent application controls because unauthorized changes may have compromised their integrity. Choice A is incorrect because the deficiency undermines all application controls running on the affected system, not just those linked to material balances.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Fraud vs error distinction in misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under auditing standards, what distinguishes a misstatement arising from fraud from a misstatement arising from error?', '["Fraud misstatements are always material while error misstatements are immaterial", "Fraud misstatements require restatement while error misstatements do not", "Fraud involves intentional action while error involves unintentional action", "Fraud misstatements affect the balance sheet while error misstatements affect the income statement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 240.02, the distinguishing factor between fraud and error is intent. Fraud involves an intentional act — whether fraudulent financial reporting or misappropriation of assets — while an error is an unintentional misstatement. Both can be material or immaterial. Choice A is incorrect because materiality is independent of intent — errors can be material and fraud can be immaterial; the classification depends on whether the act was deliberate.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Subsequent discovery of material weakness after report
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Three months after issuing an unmodified audit opinion for Cascade Manufacturing, the engagement partner learns that a material weakness existed in the company''s inventory valuation controls during the audit period. The weakness was not identified during the original audit due to an inadequate understanding of the entity''s cost allocation process. The partner should evaluate whether to:', '["Issue a revised management letter communicating the material weakness without modifying the opinion", "Disclose the deficiency in the next audit engagement letter only", "Document the deficiency in the current working papers and increase planned testing for the subsequent audit", "Assess whether the unidentified material weakness could have resulted in a material misstatement that would affect the previously issued opinion, and consider obligations under AU-C 585"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 585, when the auditor becomes aware after report release of facts that may have affected the opinion, the auditor must evaluate whether the financial statements may be materially misstated. A previously unidentified material weakness requires the auditor to assess whether it resulted in actual misstatement and whether the issued opinion remains appropriate. Choice A is incorrect because a management letter alone is insufficient — the auditor''s primary obligation is to determine whether the previously issued financial statements and opinion require revision.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Tolerable misstatement in audit sampling
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'In audit sampling, tolerable misstatement is best described as:', '["The maximum misstatement in a population that the auditor is willing to accept and still conclude the audit objective is met", "The total projected misstatement across all sampled accounts", "The amount by which performance materiality exceeds overall materiality", "The minimum sample size needed to detect material errors"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 530.05, tolerable misstatement is the application of performance materiality to a specific sampling procedure. It represents the maximum misstatement in the population that the auditor can accept while still concluding the account balance is not materially misstated. Choice D is incorrect because tolerable misstatement defines an acceptable error threshold, not a sample size calculation — sample size is derived separately based on the tolerable misstatement and expected error rate.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Management bias in estimates and misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Heritage Insurance Corp., the auditor observes that management''s estimates for claim reserves have consistently fallen at the low end of the reasonable range over the past four years, each individually immaterial. In the current year, management again selects the lowest defensible estimate, resulting in reserves $35,000 below the midpoint. Materiality is $200,000. The auditor should evaluate this pattern by:', '["Accepting each year''s estimate because it falls within the reasonable range", "Considering whether the cumulative effect of the directional bias represents a misstatement, even though each individual estimate is within the acceptable range", "Requiring management to adopt the midpoint of the range for all future estimates", "Reporting the pattern as a significant deficiency in the estimation process"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 540.A131 and AU-C 450.A17, when management''s estimates consistently fall at one end of the reasonable range, the auditor must consider whether this pattern indicates management bias. Even though each individual estimate is defensible, the cumulative directional effect may constitute a misstatement when evaluated in the aggregate. Choice A is incorrect because accepting each estimate in isolation ignores the pattern of bias — AU-C 450 requires the auditor to evaluate whether the one-directional trend indicates intentional understatement.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Effect of numerous misstatements on audit strategy
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Silverbrook Distribution, the engagement team identifies 22 misstatements across revenue, inventory, and accounts payable during substantive testing. Although the aggregate amount of $28,000 remains below the $60,000 materiality threshold, the high volume of errors was unexpected. What is the most likely effect on the auditor''s overall audit strategy?', '["The auditor should lower the materiality threshold to capture more potential errors", "The auditor should communicate all 22 misstatements individually to those charged with governance", "The auditor should reconsider the assessed level of control risk and may need to perform additional procedures", "The auditor should withdraw from the engagement due to unreliable accounting records"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.08, when the auditor identifies numerous misstatements during the audit, the auditor should reconsider whether the overall audit strategy and plan remain appropriate. A high volume of errors may indicate that control risk was assessed too low, requiring the auditor to revise risk assessments and extend substantive procedures. Choice A is incorrect because materiality is set based on user needs and financial statement benchmarks, not adjusted downward simply because many errors were found.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Immaterial misstatement indicative of fraud
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Eastgate Mortgage Services, the auditor discovers a $4,200 misstatement caused by a loan officer manually overriding system controls to process a personal loan at a below-market interest rate. Overall materiality is $150,000. What is the most likely implication of this finding for the auditor?', '["The misstatement requires a reassessment of fraud risk factors and evaluation of whether similar overrides may have occurred elsewhere, regardless of the dollar amount", "The misstatement should be accumulated on the summary of uncorrected misstatements without further investigation", "The misstatement is clearly trivial and need not be communicated to management", "The misstatement should be reported as a material weakness because it involves fraud"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 240.35, an identified misstatement resulting from fraud is not considered in isolation, even if immaterial. The auditor must evaluate the implications, including reconsidering fraud risk assessments and determining whether similar overrides may exist elsewhere. The intentional nature of the override elevates its significance beyond the dollar amount. Choice B is incorrect because simply accumulating the misstatement fails to address the fraud implications — AU-C 240 requires the auditor to respond to identified fraud by reassessing risks and expanding procedures.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Communication of deficiencies below significant level
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After completing the audit of Trailmark Logistics, the auditor identifies several control deficiencies that do not individually or collectively rise to the level of significant deficiencies or material weaknesses. The auditor believes management would benefit from knowing about these findings. Under AU-C 265, what is the most likely effect on the auditor''s communication obligations?', '["The auditor has no obligation and is prohibited from communicating deficiencies below the significant level", "The auditor must include all deficiencies in the written communication to those charged with governance", "The auditor must communicate these lesser deficiencies only if management specifically requests them", "The auditor may communicate these other deficiencies to management, either orally or in writing, and is not required to do so"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A14, the auditor may communicate control deficiencies that are not significant deficiencies or material weaknesses to management if the auditor believes they merit attention. This communication is optional and may be made orally or in writing. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 does not prohibit communicating lesser deficiencies — it simply does not require such communication, leaving it to the auditor''s professional judgment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Communication timeline for material weaknesses
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During interim audit procedures for Clearwater Hospitality Group performed in September 2025, the engagement team identifies a material weakness in the cash disbursement cycle. The fiscal year ends December 31, 2025. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should communicate this material weakness in writing to those charged with governance:', '["Within 30 calendar days of the interim fieldwork completion", "No later than 60 days after the report release date, or sooner if practicable given the significance of the findings", "By the end of the fiscal year so management can address it before year-end", "Only after completing the year-end audit procedures to confirm the deficiency still exists"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.12, the auditor must communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing no later than 60 days following the report release date. However, timely communication is encouraged, especially for material weaknesses identified during interim work. Choice D is incorrect because AU-C 265 does not require the auditor to wait until year-end procedures are complete — delaying communication of a material weakness deprives management and governance of the opportunity to take corrective action.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Extrapolation of sample misstatements to population
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Northshore Building Supply, the auditor selects a statistical sample of 40 items from a population of 3,200 accounts receivable balances totaling $1,600,000. The sample reveals misstatements totaling $6,000. Under AU-C 530, the auditor should:', '["Project the $6,000 sample misstatement to the population by multiplying it by the ratio of population size to sample size, yielding a projected misstatement of $480,000", "Record only the $6,000 identified misstatement on the summary without projection", "Reduce the clearly trivial threshold to capture smaller misstatements in additional testing", "Reperform the entire accounts receivable confirmation process before projecting"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 530.13, when misstatements are found in a sample, the auditor must project them to the population. Using the ratio method ($6,000 multiplied by 3,200 divided by 40), the projected misstatement is $480,000, which represents the auditor''s best estimate of misstatement in the entire population. Choice B is incorrect because recording only the identified sample misstatement would significantly understate the likely population error — AU-C 530 requires projection to draw valid conclusions about the account balance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Control environment definition under COSO
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under the COSO framework, the control environment is best described as:', '["The specific policies and procedures that prevent and detect misstatements", "The information systems that process financial transactions", "The set of standards, processes, and structures that provide the foundation for internal control across the organization", "The monitoring activities that evaluate whether controls are operating effectively"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the COSO Internal Control — Integrated Framework, the control environment establishes the tone of the organization and provides the discipline and structure that forms the foundation for all other components of internal control. It includes integrity, ethical values, management philosophy, and governance oversight. Choice A is incorrect because specific policies and procedures describe control activities, which is a separate COSO component that operates within the foundation established by the control environment.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Management override of controls response
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Oakmont Financial Advisors, the auditor discovers that the CFO bypassed the automated three-way match control to approve $85,000 in payments to a vendor without supporting purchase orders or receiving reports. Under AU-C 240, the auditor should respond to this management override by:', '["Documenting the override as a control deficiency and performing no additional procedures", "Reducing sample sizes for other accounts since the override was isolated to one area", "Communicating the override to the audit committee but relying on management''s explanation", "Evaluating whether the override is indicative of fraud and performing additional procedures to assess whether similar transactions occurred"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 240.31, the auditor must consider the risk of management override of controls as a fraud risk in every audit. When evidence of actual override is found, the auditor should evaluate whether it indicates fraudulent intent and design additional procedures to determine the scope. Choice A is incorrect because simply documenting the override as a control deficiency underestimates the significance — AU-C 240 requires the auditor to evaluate override specifically for fraud indicators and respond with targeted testing.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Net vs gross misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Crestfield Manufacturing, the auditor finds a $90,000 overstatement in accounts receivable and a $75,000 understatement in inventory, both resulting from different causes. Overall materiality is $100,000. Management argues that the net effect of $15,000 is immaterial and no corrections are needed. The auditor should evaluate this situation by:', '["Assessing the misstatements on both a gross and net basis, recognizing that offsetting misstatements from different causes should generally not be netted because each independently affects financial statement line items and user decisions", "Accepting management''s net calculation because the $15,000 combined effect is well below materiality", "Requiring correction of only the $90,000 receivable overstatement because it exceeds materiality independently", "Reporting both misstatements as projected misstatements rather than known misstatements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A8, the auditor should not simply net misstatements from different sources. Each misstatement affects different financial statement line items and may individually mislead users. The $90,000 receivable overstatement approaches materiality on its own and distorts the balance sheet even if the inventory understatement partially offsets it on the income statement. Choice B is incorrect because netting unrelated misstatements obscures their individual impact on financial statement presentations and could hide a material misstatement in a specific account.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Control deficiency indicators of material weakness
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Summit Ridge Healthcare, the auditor identifies a control deficiency in the payroll processing cycle where no independent verification of pay rate changes occurs. Over the past year, this control processed $4.2 million in payroll. Three unauthorized rate adjustments totaling $8,500 were detected. What factor most likely indicates this deficiency should be classified as a material weakness?', '["The $8,500 in actual errors exceeds the clearly trivial threshold", "The magnitude of transactions that could be affected by the missing control far exceeds materiality, creating a reasonable possibility of material misstatement", "The deficiency has persisted for longer than one fiscal year", "Payroll is considered a high-risk account by default under auditing standards"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A11, the severity of a control deficiency depends on the magnitude of the potential misstatement, not just actual errors found. Because the payroll cycle processes $4.2 million annually without independent verification, there is a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement could occur and not be detected. Choice A is incorrect because the actual dollar amount of detected errors is only one factor — the evaluation must focus on what could go wrong given the volume of transactions exposed to the deficiency.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Disclosure misstatements qualitative assessment
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Glenbrook Energy Corp., the auditor identifies that the related-party transaction disclosure omits $120,000 in consulting fees paid to the CEO''s spouse. Management argues the omission is immaterial because the amount is only 0.3% of total revenue. Overall materiality is $500,000. The auditor should assess this omission by evaluating:', '["Only the quantitative significance of the $120,000 relative to overall materiality", "Whether the omission can be offset against other immaterial overstatements in the financial statements", "Both the quantitative amount and the qualitative nature of the omission, because related-party disclosures are inherently significant to users regardless of dollar amount", "Whether the omission was intentional, and if not, treating it as clearly trivial"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A6 and AU-C 550.20, misstatements in disclosures must be evaluated for both quantitative and qualitative significance. Related-party transactions are inherently sensitive to financial statement users because they may indicate conflicts of interest or self-dealing. The omission of payments to the CEO''s spouse is qualitatively material regardless of its percentage of revenue. Choice A is incorrect because purely quantitative analysis fails to capture the nature of related-party disclosures — users rely on these disclosures to assess management integrity and governance quality.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Reperformance as response to control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After identifying a significant deficiency in Maple Creek Distributors'' bank reconciliation process, the auditor determines that the monthly reconciliations were not reviewed by a second person for five consecutive months. Under AU-C 330, the auditor should respond by:', '["Accepting the reconciliations as prepared because they are management''s responsibility", "Issuing a qualified opinion based on the scope limitation", "Requesting management to retroactively review all five reconciliations", "Performing substantive procedures, such as independent reconciliation or detailed cash testing, to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence for the affected period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 330.08, when the auditor identifies that a control is not operating effectively, the auditor should design and perform substantive procedures responsive to the assessed risks. Because the review control was absent for five months, the auditor cannot rely on it and must obtain evidence through alternative substantive testing. Choice B is incorrect because a control deficiency does not create a scope limitation — the auditor can still obtain sufficient evidence through substantive procedures without restricting the opinion.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Control risk reassessment due to detected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Granite Peak Electronics, the engagement team initially assessed control risk for inventory as low based on interim testing. However, during year-end substantive procedures, the team discovers eight inventory pricing misstatements totaling $47,000 in a sample of 60 items. Performance materiality is $55,000. What is the most likely effect on the auditor''s planned procedures?', '["The auditor should reassess control risk as higher than initially evaluated and consider expanding sample sizes or performing additional substantive procedures", "The auditor should accept the results because the $47,000 is below performance materiality", "The auditor should lower the materiality threshold to detect additional misstatements", "The auditor should communicate the findings to governance and issue a disclaimer of opinion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 330.25, when audit results differ significantly from expectations, the auditor must reconsider risk assessments. Eight misstatements in a sample of 60 items suggests controls are not operating as effectively as initially assessed. The auditor should increase the assessed level of control risk and perform additional substantive procedures. Choice B is incorrect because proximity to performance materiality combined with the high error frequency indicates the initial risk assessment was likely understated, warranting expanded testing rather than acceptance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — PCAOB integrated audit material weakness reporting
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under PCAOB standards for an integrated audit of a public company, the auditor must:', '["Communicate only significant deficiencies to management in writing", "Express an opinion on the effectiveness of internal control over financial reporting and communicate material weaknesses in the audit report", "Communicate deficiencies only when they result in actual misstatements", "Provide an advisory letter recommending control improvements to the board"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under PCAOB AS 2201, in an integrated audit of a public company, the auditor issues an opinion on the effectiveness of internal control over financial reporting. Any identified material weakness must result in an adverse opinion on internal controls and be communicated in the audit report itself. Choice A is incorrect because PCAOB standards require communication of material weaknesses — not just significant deficiencies — and the communication occurs through the formal audit report, not merely a management letter.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Auditor documentation of control deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, the auditor''s documentation of control deficiency evaluation must include:', '["A list of all controls tested during the audit engagement", "Management''s written response to each deficiency identified", "The dollar amount of each misstatement caused by each deficiency", "The basis for the auditor''s determination of whether identified deficiencies are significant deficiencies or material weaknesses"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A18, the auditor must document the basis for the severity classification of each identified control deficiency, including the rationale for concluding whether a deficiency is a significant deficiency or material weakness. This supports the auditor''s professional judgment. Choice A is incorrect because the documentation requirement focuses on the evaluation and classification of identified deficiencies, not a comprehensive listing of every control tested during the engagement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Turnover of key personnel as control deficiency indicator
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During planning for the audit of Westfield Electronics, the auditor learns that the accounting manager, who was solely responsible for month-end close procedures, resigned four months ago. The position has not been filled, and duties have been distributed among three staff accountants with no formal transition documentation. Under AU-C 315, the auditor should treat this situation as:', '["A normal business event that does not affect the audit approach", "An opportunity to reduce audit fees since less documentation will need to be reviewed", "A risk factor that may indicate a control deficiency requiring reassessment of control risk over the financial close process", "Automatic grounds for issuing an adverse opinion on internal control"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 315.14, the auditor must consider changes in the entity''s personnel and operations when understanding the control environment. The loss of a key individual responsible for financial close, combined with an informal transition and no permanent replacement, indicates a potential control deficiency that increases the risk of misstatement. Choice A is incorrect because the departure of a sole preparer of month-end close procedures directly affects control effectiveness and warrants heightened scrutiny of the close process.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Clearly trivial threshold definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, the clearly trivial threshold for misstatements is defined as an amount that is:', '["Inconsequential both individually and in the aggregate, whether judged by size, nature, or circumstances", "Equal to one percent of overall materiality", "Calculated as performance materiality minus overall materiality", "The smallest misstatement that could affect the audit opinion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, clearly trivial means the misstatement is inconsequential, whether taken individually or in the aggregate and whether judged by any criteria of size, nature, or circumstances. It is not simply a smaller amount than materiality but a threshold below which items need not be accumulated. Choice D is incorrect because the clearly trivial threshold is distinct from materiality — it identifies amounts so small they cannot affect the financial statements, whereas materiality reflects amounts that could influence user decisions.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Pervasive nature of control environment deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Brookfield Capital Management, the auditor determines that the board of directors has no members with financial reporting expertise and has not met in over eight months. Management operates without oversight from those charged with governance. Under AU-C 265, what is the most likely effect of this control environment deficiency on the auditor''s evaluation?', '["The auditor should classify the deficiency as a significant deficiency because it does not directly cause financial misstatements", "The auditor should treat the deficiency as pervasive, potentially affecting all financial statement assertions, and classify it as a material weakness", "The auditor should withdraw from the engagement because an effective board is a prerequisite for an audit", "The auditor should test compensating controls before classifying the deficiency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, a deficiency in the control environment — such as ineffective governance oversight — is inherently pervasive because the control environment provides the foundation for all other internal control components. The absence of an active, financially literate board creates a reasonable possibility of undetected material misstatement across all assertions. Choice A is incorrect because control environment deficiencies need not cause direct misstatements to qualify as material weaknesses — their pervasive influence on the entire control system elevates their severity.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
